# {{title}}

An IoT OS firmware starter for {{target_name}}: {{role_text}}.

It holds the IoT OS embedded SDK {{sdk_version}}, the metric tables generated from the
device profiles below, {{project_text}}, and instructions for AI coding agents. It holds
no credentials, so it is safe to commit and to give to an AI tool.

{{profiles_list}}

## Start with AI

Open this folder with an AI coding agent, such as Claude Code, Codex, Cursor or GitHub
Copilot, and send:

> Read AGENTS.md and follow it. Start with the interview.

The agent asks about your hardware and where each value comes from, writes your answers
to `{{developer_path}}device-brief.yaml`, and asks you to confirm them before it writes
any code. It never opens your connection settings.

## Build

{{build_section}}

Until the connection settings are in `config/`, the program says where to get them and
stops without connecting.

## Connection settings

{{settings_section}} Unpack the archive into this folder: it puts the settings in
`config/`, which `.gitignore` keeps out of version control. `config/README.md` explains
the files.

## Test without hardware

The desktop simulator in `sdk/examples/desktop-simulator` builds against this starter's
`generated/` and `config/`, and publishes made-up values. Use it to check the platform
side, never as a device:

```sh
cmake -S sdk/examples/desktop-simulator -B build/simulator && cmake --build build/simulator
./build/simulator/gateway-sim{{simulator_args}}
```

## What is here

| Path | What it is | When you update |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `{{developer_path}}` | Your code: `field.c` reads the hardware{{developer_extra}} | kept |
| `config/` | Connection settings, once downloaded | kept |
| `README.md`, `.gitignore`{{project_files}} | This file, ignore rules{{project_files_text}} | kept |
| `sdk/` | The IoT OS embedded SDK | replaced |
| `generated/` | Device types and metrics from the profiles | replaced |
| `.iotos/` | The device brief schema and the porting guide | replaced |
| `AGENTS.md`, `CLAUDE.md` | Instructions for AI coding agents | replaced |
| `iotos-starter.json` | What this starter was made from | replaced |

## Updating

When a profile gets a new revision, download a new starter for the same target and role,
unpack it next to this folder, and replace only the paths `iotos-starter.json` lists in
`replaceable`, plus the manifest itself:

```sh
NEW=../{{root}}-new   # the newer starter, unpacked
grep -q '"format": 1,' "$NEW/iotos-starter.json" || { echo "The newer starter needs a newer update recipe: see its README."; exit 1; }
for path in {{replaceable}}; do
  path=${path%/}
  rm -rf "./$path" && cp -R "$NEW/$path" "./$path"
done
cp "$NEW/iotos-starter.json" iotos-starter.json
```

Your code, the device brief, the settings and the build files stay as they are. To see
what changed since the revision you had, read each metric in the new
`iotos-starter.json`: `introduced_in` and `changed_in` later than your revision mark new
and changed metrics, and `retired` lists metrics the profile no longer has. The new
starter's `{{developer_path}}field.c` has stubs for new metrics to copy into yours.
